{"type":"message","id":"f3a4ad5c","parentId":"90f93f02","timestamp":"2026-07-28T12:19:13.280Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-bc9dc4eb861eb683","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"mode\": \"deep_research\",\n  \"story_id\": \"bitkub-53m-theft-thai-sec-complaint\",\n  \"category\": \"exploits\",\n  \"wp_category_slugs\": [\"exploits\", \"sec\"],\n  \"wp_category_ids\": [2085, 2462],\n  \"topic_theme\": \"Bitkub Hid $53M Theft From Thai SEC For Five Months\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"Bitkub $53M theft\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"Thai SEC Files Criminal Complaint Against Bitkub For Concealing $53M Theft\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Bitcoin\",\n  \"chart_coin\": null,\n  \"sources_used\": [\"CryptoSlate\", \"Bitcoin Foundation\"],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://cryptoslate.com/how-a-crypto-exchange-secretly-hid-53m-in-stolen-crypto-to-prevent-a-bank-run-and-why-the-sec-is-finally-coming-for-its-directors/\",\n    \"https://bitcoinfoundation.org/news/crypto-companies-news/thai-sec-accuses-bitkub-of-concealing-51-million-hack/\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"Thailand's SEC filed a criminal complaint against Bitkub Online and two former directors on July 23, 2026.\",\n    \"Hackers stole approximately 1.7 billion baht ($53 million) in customer assets across 16 cryptocurrencies in May 2021.\",\n    \"Bitkub filed daily Form DA 1 reports from May 10 through October 30, 2021, showing no material change from the theft.\",\n    \"The SEC alleges Bitkub gave false statements and that directors made false entries in company documents.\",\n    \"Directors Sakolkorn Sakavee and Thaweesap Rawan were responsible for submitting the reports.\",\n    \"Bitkub began procuring replacement digital assets on October 31, 2021.\",\n    \"Co-founders absorbed the loss by buying the same currencies in the same quantities.\",\n    \"Bitkub claims the nondisclosure was intended to prevent a bank run.\",\n    \"The SEC confirmed customer assets were safe and complete as of September 8, 2025.\",\n    \"An inquiry official will investigate and decide whether to recommend prosecution.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"Thailand's Securities and Exchange Commission has filed a criminal complaint against Bitkub, the country's largest crypto exchange, and two former directors over reports submitted after a previously undisclosed 2021 hack. The regulator said hackers stole crypto worth about 1.7 billion baht (or approximately $51 million) from Bitkub in May 2021. Daily filings submitted between May 10 and October 30 showed no material change in the company's assets. The SEC alleged that Bitkub's reports contained false information and gave the impression that customer assets were being held normally. Sakolkorn Sakavee and Thaweesap Rawan, Bitkub's directors at the time, were responsible for submitting the reports and were named alongside the company in the complaint. Bitkub acknowledged in a statement that people responsible for its disclosures had decided not to disclose that one of the company's wallets had been stolen, saying it was intended to prevent a bank run. The exchange said co-founders later absorbed the loss by buying the same cryptocurrencies in the same amounts, though the stolen assets were never recovered. The case now goes to Thailand's Economic Crime Suppression Division for investigation.\",\n  \"partial_words\": 850\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1785241153280}}
